how dnstree found www.example.com. A, one hop at a time

1. dnstree starts at the root, the zone at the top of every name. it knows
   the addresses of the root servers from the list it starts with, and asked
   a.root-servers.net. (198.41.0.4) for www.example.com. A. the root does
   not hold the answer, but knows who runs com.: it answered with a referral
   to 1 server: a.gtld-servers.net. a resolver may keep this referral for 2
   days, so a change of nameservers takes that long to reach everyone. com.
   is not signed: the parent published no DS. nothing vouches for what is
   below it, and a validating resolver takes its answers as they come.

2. first it asked b.gtld-servers.net. (192.33.14.30), which did not answer
   within 2s, so it moved on. then it asked a.gtld-servers.net. (192.5.6.30)
   for www.example.com. A. the answer did not fit in a udp datagram (the
   server set the TC bit), so it asked again over tcp, which has no such
   limit. com. does not hold the answer, but knows who runs example.com.: it
   answered with a referral to 2 servers: a.iana-servers.net. and
   ns.outside.example. it gave addresses (glue) for 1 of them. it gave no
   address for ns.outside.example., named outside example.com.: with no
   address for it, finding one takes a lookup of its own. a resolver may
   keep it for 2 days. the chain of trust reaches example.com.: the zone
   above signed a DS record, a digest of example.com.'s key, and the key
   matched it. that is how one zone vouches for the next.

3. it asked a.iana-servers.net. (199.43.135.53), one of the 2 servers the
   referral named for example.com., for www.example.com. A. example.com.
   holds the answer: www.example.com. 3600 A 93.184.216.34. it said so with
   authority (the AA flag), since it serves the zone itself. to check
   signatures, it also asked the same server for the keys of example.com.
   (DNSKEY).

that is the end of the walk: www.example.com. A is 93.184.216.34.
